+
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+# [MP8] 🩸 Only one device per person — this is the guard that was missing
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+#
+# **Bug the user reported from a 6-player match with real players on 2026-08-18**
+# "When two people throw devices at one person at the same time, both devices end up on that person at once,
+#   and it triggers the abnormal retirement logic so both Spine Breakers stop moving"
+#
+# ### Why the follow_state guards don't catch it
+#
+# When T24 added mutual exclusion it only changed `follow_state` (`_mp8_can_take` / `_mp8_can_target`) —
+# that path is "the device chases someone down by itself". But **a player actively throwing** goes through this file's path,
+# and the vanilla callback here has **no checks at all**. If it hits a SpineBreakerPlayer it goes straight to Attached.
+# So when two people throw the devices on their backs at the same third person at once, both devices stick.
+#
+# ### Why both devices stop once they're stuck
+#
+# It takes three chained steps, and every one of them is required.
+#   1. In `mp8_bind_rpc`, `p.mp8_current_device = self` — the later device **overwrites** the earlier one,
+#      so the victim has two devices on them but only remembers one.
+#   2. Each device's activation_timer runs out on its own, **each sending a BreakSpine at the same person**,
+#      so `mp8_claim_retire()` is called twice for the same life → `_mp8_kills_started` is inflated →
+#      the retirement slot is handed out **early** (in a 6-player match it should be the 4th kill, but it may go out on the 3rd).
+#   3. When the later device enters BreakSpine it does `connect(target.spine_broken)`,
+#      while the earlier device's `break_spine_rpc` is still running (it does await 0.5 + await 0.5) —
+#      connecting a step too late means the signal has already fired, `_on_spine_broken` never arrives,
+#      and this device **hangs in BreakSpine** (that state's update/update_physics are both empty).
+#   End result — one device retired and parked normally + one device hung = what the user saw as "both Spine Breakers stopped moving".
+#
+# ### Criteria
+#
+# Uses **the same rule** as `follow_state._mp8_can_take` (that one is a contact check and this one is a throw hit,
+# and the same meaning should use the same rule). Not a player / stale reference / already dead / already carrying a device — none of these stick.
+#
+# ⚠️ When it can't stick, **don't** leave it hanging in the air. A plain return would freeze the thrown device in place, which looks worse than the original bug. So it still switches to Follow.
+#
+# 🩸 **2026-08-24 correction — this used to say "the device on his back will sooner or later be thrown off or reset after its kill", and that was wrong.**
+# Once in Follow there is no way out at all. For this person `_mp8_can_take()` is always false,
+# so the device can neither latch onto nor select them, and switching targets only happens when "someone else is nearby" —
+# with nobody around it circles him forever (reported by the user from real gameplay on 2026-08-24).
+# This is now handled by `follow_state._mp8_check_dead_end()`. On the very first frame it notices
+# the target can't be latched onto and asks the host for a new target. **The full analysis is written above that constant.**
+func _mp8_can_attach(p) -> bool :
+
+	if p == null or not is_instance_valid(p):
+		return false
+	if not (p is SpineBreakerPlayer):
+		return false
+	if not p.active:
+		return false
+	if p.has_spine_breaker:
+		return false
+	return true
+
+
 func _on_player_attach_entered(body):
 
 	if not active:
@@ -115,6 +177,19 @@ func _on_player_attach_entered(body):
 	if owner.thrown_collision_exceptions.has(body):
 		return
 
+	# [MP8] See the big block above. Vanilla goes straight to Attached here with no checks at all.
+	if not _mp8_can_attach(body):
+		if multiplayer.is_server():
+			var why: String = "dead" if not body.active else "already carrying a device"
+			print("[MP8] Spine Breaker: device %d hit %s but won't stick — %s (chasing instead)" % [
+				owner.mp8_index, String(body.name), why
+			])
+		# Won't stick, but it can't freeze in the air either — chase him instead
+		state_machine.transition_to(&"Follow", {
+			"target": body,
+		})
+		return
+
 	state_machine.transition_to(&"Attached", {
 		"target": body
 	})
